//! The rights (spec §3): one enum per object kind, and what every default set
//! mints.
//!
//! The `*Right` enums with the `static_assert`s that pin the universal low
//! byte, the two kind-blind predicates the generic paths check through, and the
//! per-kind functions that say what a freshly minted handle carries. The
//! default sets read the rights, which is why the two sections share a file.
//!
//! Reached through the `sosabi` facade; see `lib.saw`.

// =============================================================================
// Rights (spec §3)
// =============================================================================

// Rights are scoped per object kind. Each kind declares its own rights enum, so
// a bit means nothing until you know which kind's word it sits in, and dispatch
// establishes the kind before any right is checked (§3's order: table lookup ->
// object type -> op table -> rights check -> op). Two consequences:
//
//   - Kind-specific bits overlap freely across kinds. A rights word is only
//     ever interpreted against its handle's kind, so each kind owns 24 bits of
//     its own instead of all kinds sharing 32.
//   - The check is typed. `SystemObject.allows` takes a `SystemRight` and
//     nothing else, so testing a pipe right against a System handle is a
//     compile error rather than a bit comparison that quietly answers.
//
// The universal low byte. Bits 0-7 are identical in every kind's enum:
//
//   bit 0   Transfer   may be sent over a pipe: the movability capability
//   bit 1   Mint       may mint a sibling handle onto the same object
//   bits 2-7           reserved (candidates as they prove universal:
//                      wait/signal, introspect/info, revoke)
//
// The doctrine: specific rights for specific operations, per object type;
// universal bits for universal ops; there is no generic authority. A bit named
// for a vague power ("manage", "derive children") ends up borrowed by unrelated
// ops and therefore gates none of them. Every op has a bit named after the op,
// and a launcher stripping one strips exactly one authority instead of an
// unpredictable bundle.
//
// `Mint` gates `MINT_OP`, the second universal op: a handle carrying it may
// produce a sibling onto the same object, in the caller's own table, with
// rights that are the source's intersected with a keep mask. That is a
// kind-independent authority (the op never learns the kind), which is what the
// universal byte is for, and `Mint` and `Transfer` are the only bits to have
// earned a place in it.
//
// The doctrine does not mean one bit per op number. `TimerRight.Arm` gates both
// `Arm` and `Disarm`, and `WaiterRight.Attach` gates both `Add` and `Remove`,
// because each pair is one capability (the power to decide when this timer
// fires, the power to decide what this Waiter watches), and splitting one would
// produce a right that withholds nothing (a holder who may arm can silence a
// timer by arming it a century out). The unit of a right is an authority a
// holder could point at.
//
// A rights bit is spelled as a shift: `case ClockGet = 1 << 10`, not `= 1024`,
// including the low bits (`Transfer = 1 << 0`), so the whole column reads as
// bit positions. The asserts below take the same spelling, so each reads as the
// rule it enforces: `>= (1 << 8)` is "starts at bit 8".
//
// The parentheses in those asserts are load-bearing: Saw's bitwise tier binds
// below comparison, so a bare `x >= 1 << 8` is `(x >= 1) << 8`.
//
// Six bits are reserved rather than assigned because more shared operations are
// coming and a universal right added later must not have to move a kind's bits
// to make room. Generic kernel paths (pipe handle-transfer is the first) check
// universal bits without knowing the kind, which is only sound while the table
// holds, so the `static_assert`s below hold it: they stop a kind enum from
// drifting, and catch a kind-specific right placed below bit 8.

/// A right on the System object.
///
/// The low byte is the universal table above; System's own rights start at bit
/// 8. `Debug` gates the console and `Shutdown` gates stopping the machine; a
/// process holding the handle without them can name the object and do nothing
/// with it, which is the point of checking rights per op rather than per
/// object.
///
/// `ProcessSelf` gates asking the System object which Process you are. It is a
/// specific authority with its own substitution seam, as `ClockGet` is: a
/// launcher that strips this bit hands a child a System handle through which it
/// can print and tell the time and never learn its own identity, and a launcher
/// that wants to supply that identity some other way (a virtual process object
/// over IPC, the move `ClockGet` describes for a virtual clock) has a bit to
/// strip rather than an op to intercept.
///
/// `ClockGet` gates the one op that hands out a time source, and it is a right
/// rather than an ambient facility because time is a granted capability. A
/// launcher that strips this bit from a child's System handle has a process
/// that cannot read the machine's clock at all, which makes handing it a
/// virtual clock over IPC later a substitution rather than a rewrite. It is
/// deliberately not in the stripped-by-construction set (`Shutdown` and the
/// bind/alloc ops): an ordinary process needs to tell the time, and a clock
/// grants authority over nothing.
///
/// `ProcessCreate` is on System rather than on Process: process-scoped objects
/// are minted by your Process, but processes are minted by the System. The
/// process table is a machine-global resource bounded by machine facts (the
/// slab, the protection-domain reloads, ASIDs where the profile translates), so
/// its refusals and limits are System answers, while threads, events, waiters
/// and timers stay a process's own. A launcher handed a System handle stripped
/// of this bit cannot spawn, which is the attenuation that matters: with a
/// root/launcher topology, a creation right on the caller's own Process handle
/// would gate nothing real.
///
/// `SlabDonate` is the most powerful bit on this object: it hands the kernel
/// memory that no op gives back. It is its own bit rather than a reading of
/// `ProcessCreate` or `Mint` because it is a different kind of authority: those
/// spend a resource the machine already has, this one changes how much the
/// machine has, for every process, with no op to undo it. A launcher handed a
/// System handle without it can create processes and mint handles all day and
/// can never move a slab ceiling; a boot server that is meant to size the
/// machine keeps it and hands its children System siblings stripped of it.
///
/// `TierGet` is the least powerful bit here: it gates reading a word that
/// describes the machine. It has a bit at all because an ambient fact no handle
/// gates would be the one place in this ABI where a process learns something
/// about the machine without holding a capability that says it may. It supports
/// the same substitution as `ClockGet`: a launcher that means to run a child
/// under a simulated platform (a flat-tier test harness over isolated hardware,
/// or the reverse) strips this bit and answers the question itself over IPC,
/// which is a substitution rather than a rewrite only because the child had no
/// second way to ask. It grants no authority over anything and leaks no other
/// process's state, so it is in the root set and in the ordinary child mask, as
/// `ClockGet` is.
///
/// `StatsRegionMap` is the one right here that does leak other processes'
/// state, which is why it is its own bit rather than riding
/// `ProcessRight.Stats`. The per-process stats op answers one process's columns
/// through a handle onto that process, so its gate is per-target by
/// construction; the region is every slot's columns in one page, so its gate
/// has to be a separate, separately strippable statement that this caller may
/// see the whole machine's traffic. A child meant to watch itself and nothing
/// else is given a System handle without this bit and reads its own columns
/// through `ProcessRight.Stats`, which the default Process set carries; a
/// supervisor that is meant to be a `top` gets this and pays no syscall per
/// sample.
///
/// It is in the root set for `SlabDonate`'s reason (root is the process meant
/// to be able to supervise the machine) and absent from the ordinary child
/// mask, because "everything a child normally gets" must not include every
/// peer's trap counts.
///
/// `InterruptBind` gates binding an IRQ line, the strongest creation authority
/// there is: it is what separates a driver from an ordinary process. A line is
/// a machine-wide resource, so the bit is on System and not on a Process
/// handle, where a process could derive it again whatever its launcher
/// withheld (`ProcessSelf` mints the Process default set). The launcher's mask
/// on the System handle it gives a child is the lever: a launcher binds each
/// line itself and gives the Interrupt to the driver that services it, so the
/// driver holds the lines it was given and no authority to bind another. It is
/// in the root set and absent from the ordinary child mask.
public enum SystemRight: UInt32 {
    case Transfer = 1 << 0,
    case Mint = 1 << 1,
    case Debug = 1 << 8,
    case Shutdown = 1 << 9,
    case ClockGet = 1 << 10,
    case ProcessCreate = 1 << 11,
    case ProcessSelf = 1 << 12,
    case SlabDonate = 1 << 13,
    case TierGet = 1 << 14,
    case StatsRegionMap = 1 << 15,
    case InterruptBind = 1 << 16,
}

// The universal low byte, pinned. Every kind's enum repeats these two asserts
// verbatim; that repetition is the check, since it makes a drifted bit fail to
// build rather than fail in a pipe transfer years later.
static_assert((SystemRight.Transfer as UInt32) == (1 << 0),
              "universal right Transfer must be bit 0 in every kind's enum")
static_assert((SystemRight.Mint as UInt32) == (1 << 1),
              "universal right Mint must be bit 1 in every kind's enum")

// Kind-specific rights start at bit 8, leaving bits 2-7 free for universal
// rights not yet assigned. One assert per kind-specific case.
static_assert((SystemRight.Debug as UInt32) >= (1 << 8),
              "kind-specific right Debug must start at bit 8 or above")
static_assert((SystemRight.Shutdown as UInt32) >= (1 << 8),
              "kind-specific right Shutdown must start at bit 8 or above")
static_assert((SystemRight.ClockGet as UInt32) >= (1 << 8),
              "kind-specific right ClockGet must start at bit 8 or above")
static_assert((SystemRight.ProcessCreate as UInt32) >= (1 << 8),
              "kind-specific right ProcessCreate must start at bit 8 or above")
static_assert((SystemRight.ProcessSelf as UInt32) >= (1 << 8),
              "kind-specific right ProcessSelf must start at bit 8 or above")
static_assert((SystemRight.SlabDonate as UInt32) >= (1 << 8),
              "kind-specific right SlabDonate must start at bit 8 or above")
static_assert((SystemRight.TierGet as UInt32) >= (1 << 8),
              "kind-specific right TierGet must start at bit 8 or above")
static_assert((SystemRight.StatsRegionMap as UInt32) >= (1 << 8),
              "kind-specific right StatsRegionMap must start at bit 8 or above")
static_assert((SystemRight.InterruptBind as UInt32) >= (1 << 8),
              "kind-specific right InterruptBind must start at bit 8 or above")

/// A right on a Process object (§2 table row).
///
/// A narrowed Process handle binds whoever holds that handle, not the process
/// it names. `SystemOp.ProcessSelf` mints `process_rights() & keep` on every
/// call, so a process whose System handle carries `SystemRight.ProcessSelf`
/// can derive the full default set again whenever it likes. Withholding one
/// of these bits from the process itself means withholding `ProcessSelf` from
/// its System handle, and that takes every derived Process op with it.
///
/// `ThreadSelf` gates one thing: asking the process for the calling thread's
/// handle. A handle without it cannot name the calling thread.
///
/// There is no `Kill` right. Spec §8 ratifies one as the capability-gated
/// escape hatch userspace supervision needs against a wedged peer process, but
/// no op implements it (`ProcessStatusKind.Killed` is declared and
/// unreachable).
///
/// `Start` and `BootHandles` gate different roles the Process object plays:
///
///   - `Start` is a receiver authority on the child's handle: the second phase
///     of the two-phase lifecycle. It is its own bit because a supervisor may
///     rightly hold a child's handle to watch it (`Wait`) without being
///     allowed to run it.
///   - `BootHandles` gates the boot-set iterator, which drains the caller's
///     own set: root's, written by the kernel at boot, or a child's, written
///     by its launcher's gives. It is in the Process default set; a process
///     whose Process handle lacks it cannot drain what it was given, apart
///     from the handle the start tag names, whose word arrives in its first
///     argument register.
///
/// `ProcessCreate` is not here: processes are minted by the System, so it is
/// `SystemRight.ProcessCreate`.
///
/// `EventCreate` and `WaiterCreate` are the factory capability for plain
/// objects (spec §12 leaves the creation-authority model open between
/// quota-gated free creation and a factory capability; this is the factory
/// capability, and one a process already holds). The Process object is what an
/// Event or a Waiter is charged to (its slab slot and its quota row; the handle
/// is the caller's), so letting the authority to create one flow through the
/// Process handle is §3's derivation rule rather than a new mechanism beside
/// it. A
/// quota is additive on top: each creation op checks the process's quota row
/// before it asks the slab, so `QuotaExceeded` comes before `NoResource`.
///
/// There is one bit per creation op rather than a single `Create`, matching
/// `ThreadCreate`. The bits are what attenuation strips, so an object kind a
/// handle may not make is a bit that handle lacks; collapsing them would make
/// "may create Events" and "may create Waiters" the same capability, which they
/// are not.
///
/// `Map` is an authority over this process. `Memory.Map` / `IoMemory.Map` name
/// a target Process as their argument, and installing a protection row into
/// somebody's domain is not something possession of a region should imply. So a
/// map spends two rights on two objects, `MemoryRight.Map` on the region and
/// this bit on the process being mapped into, and a child's handle without
/// this bit installs nothing into that child. It sits beside `Give` for the
/// reason `Give` sits beside `Start`: they are separable authorities over one
/// child. A builder that may furnish a child's boot set need not be allowed to
/// edit its address space, and a supervisor that may run and watch need not be
/// allowed either.
///
/// `Give` is its own bit: it is the authority to place handles into this
/// child's boot set. It sits beside `Start` rather than inside it because the
/// two are separable: a supervisor may hold a child's handle to run and watch
/// it without being allowed to furnish it, and a builder may furnish without
/// being allowed to run.
///
/// `PipeCreate` gates the factory for a connection, which sits on the Process
/// object like `EventCreate` and `WaiterCreate` and unlike `ProcessCreate`:
/// pipes are IPC and carry nothing system-shaped. A handle without this bit
/// cannot make a connection, though connections somebody else made can still
/// be held and used. That narrows the handle, not the process: one whose
/// System handle carries `SystemRight.ProcessSelf` can derive a Process handle
/// with the bit again (see above). It joins the one default set below on the
/// same terms as `EventCreate`: the Process object is what a connection is
/// charged to (its slab slot and its quota row; the two handles are the
/// caller's).
///
/// `Stats` is an authority to observe. It gates `ProcessOp.Stats`, and reading
/// three counters is worth a bit of its own rather than riding `Wait`, the
/// other observation bit. `Wait` answers §8's status word, a fact about whether
/// the process is alive; `Stats` answers how many traps it has taken, a fact
/// about what it has been doing. A supervisor that may learn a child died need
/// not be able to watch it work, and the separation costs one bit. It joins the
/// one default set below because self-inspection has to compose for free
/// (`ProcessSelf` -> `proc.stats()`), and withholding it from a supervisor is
/// a keep mask on the sibling of the child's handle that supervisor holds,
/// rather than a second default list.
public enum ProcessRight: UInt32 {
    case Transfer = 1 << 0,
    case Mint = 1 << 1,
    case ThreadCreate = 1 << 8,
    case Exit = 1 << 9,
    case Wait = 1 << 10,
    case EventCreate = 1 << 11,
    case WaiterCreate = 1 << 12,
    case Start = 1 << 14,
    case BootHandles = 1 << 15,
    case ThreadSelf = 1 << 16,
    case Give = 1 << 17,
    case Map = 1 << 18,
    case PipeCreate = 1 << 19,
    case Stats = 1 << 20,
}

static_assert((ProcessRight.Transfer as UInt32) == (1 << 0),
              "universal right Transfer must be bit 0 in every kind's enum")
static_assert((ProcessRight.Mint as UInt32) == (1 << 1),
              "universal right Mint must be bit 1 in every kind's enum")
static_assert((ProcessRight.ThreadCreate as UInt32) >= (1 << 8),
              "kind-specific right ThreadCreate must start at bit 8 or above")
static_assert((ProcessRight.Exit as UInt32) >= (1 << 8),
              "kind-specific right Exit must start at bit 8 or above")
static_assert((ProcessRight.Wait as UInt32) >= (1 << 8),
              "kind-specific right Wait must start at bit 8 or above")
static_assert((ProcessRight.EventCreate as UInt32) >= (1 << 8),
              "kind-specific right EventCreate must start at bit 8 or above")
static_assert((ProcessRight.WaiterCreate as UInt32) >= (1 << 8),
              "kind-specific right WaiterCreate must start at bit 8 or above")
static_assert((ProcessRight.Start as UInt32) >= (1 << 8),
              "kind-specific right Start must start at bit 8 or above")
static_assert((ProcessRight.BootHandles as UInt32) >= (1 << 8),
              "kind-specific right BootHandles must start at bit 8 or above")
static_assert((ProcessRight.ThreadSelf as UInt32) >= (1 << 8),
              "kind-specific right ThreadSelf must start at bit 8 or above")
static_assert((ProcessRight.Give as UInt32) >= (1 << 8),
              "kind-specific right Give must start at bit 8 or above")
static_assert((ProcessRight.Map as UInt32) >= (1 << 8),
              "kind-specific right Map must start at bit 8 or above")
static_assert((ProcessRight.PipeCreate as UInt32) >= (1 << 8),
              "kind-specific right PipeCreate must start at bit 8 or above")
static_assert((ProcessRight.Stats as UInt32) >= (1 << 8),
              "kind-specific right Stats must start at bit 8 or above")

/// A right on a Thread object (§2 table row).
///
/// The split is between what an owner of a thread may do to it (`Start` it,
/// `Join` it) and what a thread does to itself, which is `Control`: exit, and
/// yield the processor. Both of the latter check that the caller is the named
/// thread, so `Control` on someone else's handle still cannot drive it; the
/// right is what makes the check worth running rather than the check itself.
public enum ThreadRight: UInt32 {
    case Transfer = 1 << 0,
    case Mint = 1 << 1,
    case Start = 1 << 8,
    case Join = 1 << 9,
    case Control = 1 << 10,
}

static_assert((ThreadRight.Transfer as UInt32) == (1 << 0),
              "universal right Transfer must be bit 0 in every kind's enum")
static_assert((ThreadRight.Mint as UInt32) == (1 << 1),
              "universal right Mint must be bit 1 in every kind's enum")
static_assert((ThreadRight.Start as UInt32) >= (1 << 8),
              "kind-specific right Start must start at bit 8 or above")
static_assert((ThreadRight.Join as UInt32) >= (1 << 8),
              "kind-specific right Join must start at bit 8 or above")
static_assert((ThreadRight.Control as UInt32) >= (1 << 8),
              "kind-specific right Control must start at bit 8 or above")

/// A right on an Event object (§2.4).
///
/// The three are the three ends of a notification and they attenuate apart on
/// purpose. `Signal` is the producer's: it is the whole of what a driver or a
/// peer needs, and a handle carrying only this bit can raise the event and can
/// never learn anything from it. `Receive` is the consumer's: it drains the
/// word and resets it. `Wait` is the right to have readiness observed, what
/// attaching to a Waiter spends, and it is separate from `Receive` because a
/// dispatcher that parks on a set of events need not be the code that reads any
/// of them.
///
/// `Wait` is a consuming right too: a wait's record carries the accumulated
/// word and the delivery clears it, so a handle holding only this bit takes
/// values as well as observes them. The two bits therefore attenuate by door
/// (park or poll) rather than by whether the holder learns anything, and a
/// launcher that wants a pure readiness observer has no bit for it.
///
/// `Mint` is granted at creation on the uniform lean the default-sets section
/// records: a second name for an Event costs nothing and can itself be
/// attenuated, where withholding it would only mean the object cannot be handed
/// on in narrowed form.
public enum EventRight: UInt32 {
    case Transfer = 1 << 0,
    case Mint = 1 << 1,
    case Signal = 1 << 8,
    case Receive = 1 << 9,
    case Wait = 1 << 10,
}

static_assert((EventRight.Transfer as UInt32) == (1 << 0),
              "universal right Transfer must be bit 0 in every kind's enum")
static_assert((EventRight.Mint as UInt32) == (1 << 1),
              "universal right Mint must be bit 1 in every kind's enum")
static_assert((EventRight.Signal as UInt32) >= (1 << 8),
              "kind-specific right Signal must start at bit 8 or above")
static_assert((EventRight.Receive as UInt32) >= (1 << 8),
              "kind-specific right Receive must start at bit 8 or above")
static_assert((EventRight.Wait as UInt32) >= (1 << 8),
              "kind-specific right Wait must start at bit 8 or above")

/// A right on a Waiter object (§2.2).
///
/// `Attach` gates both `Add` and `Remove`: one bit for what reads like two
/// operations, because they are one capability, the power to decide what a
/// Waiter watches. A holder who may add may already replace the whole
/// attachment set by adding and never removing, so splitting the pair would
/// produce a right that withholds nothing.
///
/// `Wait` is the right to park on it, and it is separate because the two roles
/// divide: §2.2 makes many threads waiting on one Waiter legal, so a pool of
/// workers holds `Wait` while whoever builds the attachment set holds `Attach`.
public enum WaiterRight: UInt32 {
    case Transfer = 1 << 0,
    case Mint = 1 << 1,
    case Attach = 1 << 8,
    case Wait = 1 << 9,
}

static_assert((WaiterRight.Transfer as UInt32) == (1 << 0),
              "universal right Transfer must be bit 0 in every kind's enum")
static_assert((WaiterRight.Mint as UInt32) == (1 << 1),
              "universal right Mint must be bit 1 in every kind's enum")
static_assert((WaiterRight.Attach as UInt32) >= (1 << 8),
              "kind-specific right Attach must start at bit 8 or above")
static_assert((WaiterRight.Wait as UInt32) >= (1 << 8),
              "kind-specific right Wait must start at bit 8 or above")

/// A right on an Interrupt object (§2 table; spec §9).
///
/// Two rights, the two halves of spec §9's cycle. `Wait` is the right to have
/// the line's assertion observed (what attaching to a Waiter spends, the same
/// meaning `EventRight.Wait` has) and `Ack` is the right to end a fire, which
/// unmasks the line and lets the device interrupt again. They attenuate apart
/// for a real shape: a supervisor that watches a driver's lines holds `Wait`
/// alone and can never rearm one, which is exactly the authority a monitor
/// should have.
///
/// There is deliberately no `Mask` right and no mask op. Masking is the
/// kernel's half of the cycle (§9's mask-on-fire), not something a driver asks
/// for: a driver that could mask a line at will could also leave it masked,
/// which is a denial of the device with no diagnostic. What a driver controls
/// is its own device's interrupt-enable register, through the window it was
/// granted, which is where that decision belongs.
///
/// `Transfer` is how the one handle reaches the driver that services the line.
/// `Mint` is declared and never handed out: an Interrupt has one name, its
/// handle, so there is no sibling to mint, and attenuation is monotonic, so no
/// handle can regain the bit. Narrowing a handle before it travels is a give's
/// keep mask (a monitor given `Wait` alone cannot rearm the line).
public enum InterruptRight: UInt32 {
    case Transfer = 1 << 0,
    case Mint = 1 << 1,
    case Wait = 1 << 8,
    case Ack = 1 << 9,
}

static_assert((InterruptRight.Transfer as UInt32) == (1 << 0),
              "universal right Transfer must be bit 0 in every kind's enum")
static_assert((InterruptRight.Mint as UInt32) == (1 << 1),
              "universal right Mint must be bit 1 in every kind's enum")
static_assert((InterruptRight.Wait as UInt32) >= (1 << 8),
              "kind-specific right Wait must start at bit 8 or above")
static_assert((InterruptRight.Ack as UInt32) >= (1 << 8),
              "kind-specific right Ack must start at bit 8 or above")

/// A right on a Clock object (§2 table).
///
/// The two split reading time from scheduling it, and they attenuate apart for
/// a shape that already exists: a process that only ever stamps a log line
/// holds `Read` alone and can never consume a Timer slot, while a dispatcher
/// that arms timeouts holds both. Neither is the other's subset in practice:
/// `TimerCreate` without `Read` is a process that sleeps by the clock without
/// being able to name what time it is, which is what a pure event loop does.
///
/// `Mint` is granted at creation on the uniform lean the default-sets section
/// records.
public enum ClockRight: UInt32 {
    case Transfer = 1 << 0,
    case Mint = 1 << 1,
    case Read = 1 << 8,
    case TimerCreate = 1 << 9,
}

static_assert((ClockRight.Transfer as UInt32) == (1 << 0),
              "universal right Transfer must be bit 0 in every kind's enum")
static_assert((ClockRight.Mint as UInt32) == (1 << 1),
              "universal right Mint must be bit 1 in every kind's enum")
static_assert((ClockRight.Read as UInt32) >= (1 << 8),
              "kind-specific right Read must start at bit 8 or above")
static_assert((ClockRight.TimerCreate as UInt32) >= (1 << 8),
              "kind-specific right TimerCreate must start at bit 8 or above")

/// A right on a Timer object (§2 table).
///
/// `Arm` gates both `Arm` and `Disarm`: one bit for what reads like two
/// operations, the same call `WaiterRight.Attach` makes and for the same
/// reason. They are one capability, the power to decide when this timer fires,
/// and a holder who may arm can already silence a timer by arming it a century
/// out, so splitting the pair would produce a right that withholds nothing.
///
/// `Wait` is the right to have readiness observed, what attaching to a Waiter
/// spends, the same meaning it has on an Event and an Interrupt. It is separate
/// because the roles divide: a supervisor parked on a set of timeouts holds
/// `Wait` alone and can never reschedule one.
///
/// `Mint` is granted at creation on the uniform lean the default-sets section
/// records.
public enum TimerRight: UInt32 {
    case Transfer = 1 << 0,
    case Mint = 1 << 1,
    case Arm = 1 << 8,
    case Wait = 1 << 9,
}

static_assert((TimerRight.Transfer as UInt32) == (1 << 0),
              "universal right Transfer must be bit 0 in every kind's enum")
static_assert((TimerRight.Mint as UInt32) == (1 << 1),
              "universal right Mint must be bit 1 in every kind's enum")
static_assert((TimerRight.Arm as UInt32) >= (1 << 8),
              "kind-specific right Arm must start at bit 8 or above")
static_assert((TimerRight.Wait as UInt32) >= (1 << 8),
              "kind-specific right Wait must start at bit 8 or above")

/// A right on a Memory object (spec §2.5).
///
/// `Transfer` lets a launcher `give` a region to a child, and `Mint` makes an
/// attenuated sibling. Both are minted at boot because attenuation is
/// monotonic: a bit not minted at boot could never appear later.
///
/// The kind-specific bits are named for their ops:
///
///   - `Split` is the authority to derive: one cut from the front, the parent
///     becoming the remainder. A pool root a launcher hands out with this bit
///     withheld is a fixed range and nothing else: "here is your RAM, you may
///     not subdivide it for anyone".
///   - `Map` is the authority to install this region in some domain. It is half
///     of what a map spends: `ProcessRight.Map` on the target process is the
///     other half, because possession of bytes must not imply authority over an
///     address space.
///
/// There is deliberately no `Read`/`Write` pair. Access is a property of the
/// mapping (`map(access:)`), which is what lets one region be mapped read-only
/// in one place and read-write in another. Rights on the region could not
/// express that without becoming per-mapping, at which point they would be the
/// mapping's.
///
/// `MapExecute` is the one exception. Execute stays a property of the mapping
/// (a region carries no R/W/X triple), but which access bits a handle may
/// request is itself an authority: `MemoryOp.Map` refuses `MapAccess.Execute`
/// unless the handle carries this bit, the same refusal every unspent right
/// answers.
///
/// It is capability flow, not identity. "Only root maps executable" holds
/// because root never grants the bit: it mints an attenuated sibling and gives
/// that, and the kernel never asks who is calling. A launcher that hands a
/// driver child its RAM through a mint whose keep mask omits `MapExecute` has
/// handed over memory that child can read and write and can never execute from,
/// stated once at the mint rather than checked at every map.
///
/// It is a bit rather than a W^X rule alone because the two answer different
/// questions. W^X (in `MapAccess`) says no single row may be both writable and
/// executable, which every process may still satisfy by double-mapping. This
/// bit says whether a process may produce an executable row at all. A system
/// that only had W^X would let any holder of a region build itself a JIT; a
/// system that only had this bit would let a privileged holder build a W|X row.
/// Both, and each is one comparison.
///
/// It is per-handle, not per-region: a second handle onto the same bytes that
/// still carries the bit can map them executable. There is no aliasing
/// bookkeeping, and a region-level immutable flag would be a second mechanism
/// doing overlapping work.
public enum MemoryRight: UInt32 {
    case Transfer = 1 << 0,
    case Mint = 1 << 1,
    case Split = 1 << 8,
    case Map = 1 << 9,
    case MapExecute = 1 << 10,
}

static_assert((MemoryRight.Transfer as UInt32) == (1 << 0),
              "universal right Transfer must be bit 0 in every kind's enum")
static_assert((MemoryRight.Mint as UInt32) == (1 << 1),
              "universal right Mint must be bit 1 in every kind's enum")
static_assert((MemoryRight.Split as UInt32) >= (1 << 8),
              "kind-specific right Split must start at bit 8 or above")
static_assert((MemoryRight.Map as UInt32) >= (1 << 8),
              "kind-specific right Map must start at bit 8 or above")
static_assert((MemoryRight.MapExecute as UInt32) >= (1 << 8),
              "kind-specific right MapExecute must start at bit 8 or above")

/// A right on an IoMemory object (spec §2.5).
///
/// The same two shapes as `MemoryRight`, with the derivation verb changed, and
/// the change of verb is the whole difference between the kinds. `Carve` takes
/// a sub-window and leaves the parent alone (§2.5: "a fixed region may be
/// handed out many times"), where `Split` consumes what it hands out. No bit
/// means both, because a holder of one authority is not a holder of the other:
/// a driver handed a carve right over a peripheral's window can produce views
/// for its own sub-devices without ever being able to take a range away from
/// anyone.
///
/// `Map` means what it means on a Memory and spends the same second right
/// (`ProcessRight.Map`) on the target. What differs is the row it installs: an
/// IoMemory can only ever produce a device-attribute row, which is the
/// type-level guarantee `ObjType.IoMemory` exists for.
///
/// `Transfer` is here because handing a driver its window is the driver-child
/// flow (`Process.give(iomemory:)` moves the register page into a child's table
/// and the child maps it into itself), and `Mint` on the uniform lean the
/// default-sets section records. There is deliberately no `MapExecute` bit
/// beside `Map`: `IoMemoryOp.Map` takes no access argument at all, so there is
/// nothing to gate.
public enum IoMemoryRight: UInt32 {
    case Transfer = 1 << 0,
    case Mint = 1 << 1,
    case Carve = 1 << 8,
    case Map = 1 << 9,
}

static_assert((IoMemoryRight.Transfer as UInt32) == (1 << 0),
              "universal right Transfer must be bit 0 in every kind's enum")
static_assert((IoMemoryRight.Mint as UInt32) == (1 << 1),
              "universal right Mint must be bit 1 in every kind's enum")
static_assert((IoMemoryRight.Carve as UInt32) >= (1 << 8),
              "kind-specific right Carve must start at bit 8 or above")
static_assert((IoMemoryRight.Map as UInt32) >= (1 << 8),
              "kind-specific right Map must start at bit 8 or above")

/// A right on a Mapping object (spec §2.5).
///
/// `Unmap` is the authority to take the row out, and §2.5's "only the Mapping
/// handle can unmap" is what makes holding one meaningful at all. `Base` is the
/// authority to ask where the row is.
///
/// `Transfer` is declared and never minted. The bit exists because the
/// universal low byte is pinned in every kind's enum by the asserts below, but
/// `mapping_rights()` does not grant it, so a Mapping cannot be given and
/// `BootHandleKind` has no case for one. A Mapping names a row in a specific
/// process's domain, so handing one to a third party would hand over the power
/// to edit an address space that party was never given authority over.
/// Revocation across a process boundary is a supervision story that would want
/// its own design rather than a bit granted by default.
public enum MappingRight: UInt32 {
    case Transfer = 1 << 0,
    case Mint = 1 << 1,
    case Unmap = 1 << 8,
    /// May this handle ask where the row is?
    ///
    /// A separate bit from `Unmap` because the two are separable authorities
    /// and a mask should be able to say so: a launcher may hand a child a
    /// Mapping it can locate and not revoke (the ordinary shape: the child
    /// needs the address to use the memory), or one it can revoke and not
    /// locate. Reading an address is not the same power as taking the memory
    /// away, and folding them into one bit would make every mask coarser than
    /// the fail-closed rule wants.
    case Base = 1 << 9,
}

static_assert((MappingRight.Transfer as UInt32) == (1 << 0),
              "universal right Transfer must be bit 0 in every kind's enum")
static_assert((MappingRight.Mint as UInt32) == (1 << 1),
              "universal right Mint must be bit 1 in every kind's enum")
static_assert((MappingRight.Unmap as UInt32) >= (1 << 8),
              "kind-specific right Unmap must start at bit 8 or above")
static_assert((MappingRight.Base as UInt32) >= (1 << 8),
              "kind-specific right Base must start at bit 8 or above")

/// A right on a PipeInlet, the client end of a connection (spec §2.1).
///
/// `Post` gates the one submission op the kernel's client side has. There is
/// exactly one because `post` is the primitive: blocking send, timeout and tell
/// are library compositions over it, so there is no second door for a bit to
/// gate.
///
/// `Wait` gates attaching this end to a Waiter, observing its room-to-post
/// level.
///
/// `Transfer` is minted because handing a child one end of a connection is the
/// point of the pair, and `Mint` on the uniform lean the default-sets section
/// records: a second name for an endpoint is a second reference on that
/// endpoint's column, which makes "any handle to an end holds that end open"
/// (the Unix `dup` precedent) true by construction rather than by a check.
public enum PipeInletRight: UInt32 {
    case Transfer = 1 << 0,
    case Mint = 1 << 1,
    case Post = 1 << 8,
    /// Gates attaching this kind to a Waiter, on the Timer precedent. The
    /// outlet's readable level and this end's own room-to-post level are two
    /// different waitables; this bit gates attaching this end (spec §2.2's
    /// waitable list). It is in the default set for the reason every
    /// kind-specific bit is: attenuation is monotonic, and a bit not minted at
    /// creation could never appear later.
    case Wait = 1 << 9,
}

static_assert((PipeInletRight.Transfer as UInt32) == (1 << 0),
              "universal right Transfer must be bit 0 in every kind's enum")
static_assert((PipeInletRight.Mint as UInt32) == (1 << 1),
              "universal right Mint must be bit 1 in every kind's enum")
static_assert((PipeInletRight.Post as UInt32) >= (1 << 8),
              "kind-specific right Post must start at bit 8 or above")
static_assert((PipeInletRight.Wait as UInt32) >= (1 << 8),
              "kind-specific right Wait must start at bit 8 or above")

/// A right on a PipeOutlet, the server end of a connection (spec §2.1).
///
/// The mirror of `PipeInletRight` with the verb changed, and the change of verb
/// is the whole difference between the ends: `Take` gates the one op that reads
/// a staged message out. What `PipeInletRight` says about `Wait`, `Transfer`
/// and `Mint` holds here word for word.
///
/// The bits overlap `PipeInletRight`'s and that is sound: a rights word is only
/// ever interpreted against its handle's kind, which §3's order establishes
/// first, so `Post` and `Take` sharing bit 8 is the per-kind scoping working
/// rather than a collision. Testing an inlet's right against an outlet is a
/// compile error.
public enum PipeOutletRight: UInt32 {
    case Transfer = 1 << 0,
    case Mint = 1 << 1,
    case Take = 1 << 8,
    /// Gates attaching this kind to a Waiter, on the Timer precedent. Attaching
    /// the server end is what a multiplexed server does instead of polling
    /// `Take` (spec §2.2's waitable list). It is in the default set for the
    /// reason every kind-specific bit is: attenuation is monotonic, and a bit
    /// not minted at creation could never appear later.
    case Wait = 1 << 9,
}

static_assert((PipeOutletRight.Transfer as UInt32) == (1 << 0),
              "universal right Transfer must be bit 0 in every kind's enum")
static_assert((PipeOutletRight.Mint as UInt32) == (1 << 1),
              "universal right Mint must be bit 1 in every kind's enum")
static_assert((PipeOutletRight.Take as UInt32) >= (1 << 8),
              "kind-specific right Take must start at bit 8 or above")
static_assert((PipeOutletRight.Wait as UInt32) >= (1 << 8),
              "kind-specific right Wait must start at bit 8 or above")

/// A right on a PipeReply, the client's claim on one reply (spec §2.1).
///
/// `Resolve` gates the one op a claim carries. `Wait` is the reply-ready level
/// §2.2 lists, and it is the sharpest: a claim's delivery carries the reply's
/// own bytes, so attaching one replaces resolving it.
///
/// `Transfer` is minted because a claim is a capability like any other and §2.1
/// makes both one-shots transferable.
///
/// `Mint` is not minted, and a one-shot is the one place the uniform lean
/// breaks. The bit is declared here (universal bit 1 belongs in every kind's
/// enum whatever any default set does with it, which the `static_assert` below
/// pins) and `pipe_reply_rights()` simply does not hand it out. Two things
/// decided it. Attenuate-then-give is `give`'s keep mask
/// (`tests/give-keep-mask`), a claim has two meaningful bits to attenuate
/// between, and no caller mints a one-shot sibling. And a sibling undermines
/// the single-use `Resolve`: `resolve` consumes on every path and parks when
/// pending, so a second name is a second claim that can only ever be told
/// `PeerClosed`, a nuisance dressed as authority. Attenuation is monotonic, so
/// the absence is permanent: `Post` mints the one name and `Transfer` moves it.
public enum PipeReplyRight: UInt32 {
    case Transfer = 1 << 0,
    case Mint = 1 << 1,
    case Resolve = 1 << 8,
    /// Gates attaching this kind to a Waiter, on the Timer precedent. Attaching
    /// a claim is how a client waits for one answer among many, and the
    /// delivery carries the answer itself, so the attach can replace the
    /// resolve entirely. It is in the default set for the reason every
    /// kind-specific bit is: attenuation is monotonic, and a bit not minted at
    /// creation could never appear later.
    case Wait = 1 << 9,
}

static_assert((PipeReplyRight.Transfer as UInt32) == (1 << 0),
              "universal right Transfer must be bit 0 in every kind's enum")
static_assert((PipeReplyRight.Mint as UInt32) == (1 << 1),
              "universal right Mint must be bit 1 in every kind's enum")
static_assert((PipeReplyRight.Resolve as UInt32) >= (1 << 8),
              "kind-specific right Resolve must start at bit 8 or above")
static_assert((PipeReplyRight.Wait as UInt32) >= (1 << 8),
              "kind-specific right Wait must start at bit 8 or above")

/// A right on a PipeRequest, the server's obligation to send one reply (spec
/// §2.1).
///
/// The mirror of `PipeReplyRight` with the verb changed, and the change of verb
/// is the whole difference between the two halves of a pair: a claim resolves,
/// an obligation replies. What `PipeReplyRight` says about `Wait` (here it
/// gates the request-abandoned level, §2.1's early notice), about `Transfer`
/// (here it is §2.1's delegation primitive rather than a convenience) and about
/// `Mint` (declared, not minted, by the one-shot-has-one-name rule) holds word
/// for word.
///
/// The bits overlap `PipeReplyRight`'s and that is sound for the reason the
/// endpoint pair's do: a rights word is only ever read against its handle's
/// kind, which §3's order establishes first.
public enum PipeRequestRight: UInt32 {
    case Transfer = 1 << 0,
    case Mint = 1 << 1,
    case Reply = 1 << 8,
    /// Gates attaching this kind to a Waiter, on the Timer precedent. Attaching
    /// an obligation is the server's cancellation watcher: §2.1's "the
    /// `PipeRequestHandle` is waitable for abandoned". It is in the default set
    /// for the reason every kind-specific bit is: attenuation is monotonic, and
    /// a bit not minted at creation could never appear later.
    case Wait = 1 << 9,
}

static_assert((PipeRequestRight.Transfer as UInt32) == (1 << 0),
              "universal right Transfer must be bit 0 in every kind's enum")
static_assert((PipeRequestRight.Mint as UInt32) == (1 << 1),
              "universal right Mint must be bit 1 in every kind's enum")
static_assert((PipeRequestRight.Reply as UInt32) >= (1 << 8),
              "kind-specific right Reply must start at bit 8 or above")
static_assert((PipeRequestRight.Wait as UInt32) >= (1 << 8),
              "kind-specific right Wait must start at bit 8 or above")

/// Whether a rights word carries the universal `Transfer` bit (§3's low byte).
///
/// It takes a plain mask rather than a typed right, on purpose.
/// `ProcessOp.Give` checks this bit on the handle being given, which may be any
/// kind at all, so the check happens at the one altitude that has the entry and
/// has not branched on its `obj_type`, as `RELEASE_OP` does. A typed `allows`
/// cannot be written there, and writing one per kind would be many spellings of
/// one universal bit.
///
/// `SystemRight.Transfer` is the spelling and any kind's would do: the
/// `static_assert`s above pin every enum's `Transfer` to bit 0, and that
/// repetition is what makes this function's one reading safe.
public func rights_allow_transfer(rights: UInt32) -> Bool {
    (rights & (SystemRight.Transfer as UInt32)) != 0
}

/// Whether a rights word carries the universal `Mint` bit (§3's low byte, bit
/// 1).
///
/// The same shape and reasoning as `rights_allow_transfer` above: `MINT_OP` is
/// intercepted before the kind match, so the check has the entry and not the
/// kind, and a typed `allows` cannot be written there. `SystemRight.Mint` is
/// the spelling and any kind's would do: the `static_assert`s pin every kind to
/// bit 1.
public func rights_allow_mint(rights: UInt32) -> Bool {
    (rights & (SystemRight.Mint as UInt32)) != 0
}

// =============================================================================
// What every default set mints (spec §3)
// =============================================================================
//
// `Mint` is in every default set but the two one-shots'. The lean is
// uniformity: the alternative is a per-kind table nobody can predict from the
// outside, so a handle either can name its object twice or cannot, and making
// that vary by kind means a launcher has to know which kinds are mintable
// before it can write a policy.
//
// What breaks the uniformity is single use, and only that.
// `pipe_reply_rights()` and `pipe_request_rights()` withhold the bit because a
// second name on a one-shot cannot mean what a second name means anywhere else:
// the object is spent by one op, so the sibling is not a second way to use the
// object but a handle whose only possible answer is `PeerClosed`. The enum bits
// stay declared, so the universal-bit-1 table is untouched.
//
// Granting `Mint` everywhere else costs nothing. A mint amplifies nothing (§3):
// the rights it produces are the source's intersected with a keep mask, so the
// sibling is always a subset, and the authority to mint is itself a bit a mask
// can omit, so a launcher hands out a handle whose mask left `Mint` out and the
// proliferation stops there. A default that withheld `Mint` would only mean
// "this kind cannot be attenuated", a strictly worse system: the alternative to
// minting an attenuated sibling is handing over the full-rights original.
//
// Where `Mint` is withheld is a per-kind ruling, as `Transfer`'s placement is,
// and the two one-shots are the whole of the list.

/// What the kernel mints for root's System handle at boot (§12).
///
/// A function rather than a `static`: the body would be a legal static
/// initializer, but a bare magic number with a comment saying which bits it is
/// made of is exactly what this file avoids.
///
/// Root gets every `SystemRight`. Among them are the derivation bits its boot
/// set is a seed of rather than the whole of: `ProcessSelf` (ask the System
/// object which Process you are), `ClockGet` (ask for a time source) and
/// `Mint`. Every later object arrives through a handle already held (§3's
/// derivation rule) rather than through a register the kernel had to widen.
///
/// `Transfer` is here so root can launch children. Root mints a masked sibling
/// of its System handle (for example `Debug | ProcessSelf | ClockGet |
/// Transfer`, with `Shutdown`, `ProcessCreate` and `Mint` absent by silence
/// from the keep mask) and gives that to a child. So a child bootstraps exactly
/// as root does: a System handle in its first argument register, its own
/// Process handle derived from it, and its boot set drained from there (§12's
/// symmetry). It can print without owning a device and tell the time, and can
/// neither stop the machine nor spawn.
///
/// Note which handle travels: never this one. Root gives a sibling a keep mask
/// has already narrowed, and what a child receives cannot be re-widened, since
/// a mint's result is a subset of its source.
///
/// The sibling must keep `Transfer`: `give` checks the universal `Transfer`
/// bit on the handle being given. What arrives need not keep it, because the
/// give's own keep mask narrows the child's entry. A child left holding
/// `Transfer` could pass the handle on only by a give, which is inert while it
/// has no `ProcessCreate` to make a receiver with.
///
/// `ProcessCreate` is a bit on the System handle the entry register carries:
/// processes are minted by the System, so root's authority to launch lives on
/// the one handle the kernel always passes. A launcher handed a System handle
/// without it cannot spawn.
///
/// Attenuation is monotonic, so a right withheld here can never appear later,
/// which is the right default for a set the boot handle hands out.
public func root_system_rights() -> UInt32 {
    // `SlabDonate` mints in the root set of necessity: §3's attenuation is
    // monotonic, so a right nothing starts with is a right nothing can ever
    // hold. Root is init and its authority is the machine (the same argument
    // that makes its quota rows unlimited), and a deployment that does not want
    // its children sizing slabs strips the bit on the way down.
    ((SystemRight.Debug as UInt32) | (SystemRight.Shutdown as UInt32)
     | (SystemRight.ProcessSelf as UInt32) | (SystemRight.ClockGet as UInt32)
     | (SystemRight.ProcessCreate as UInt32) | (SystemRight.Mint as UInt32)
     | (SystemRight.SlabDonate as UInt32)
     // `TierGet` is here on `ClockGet`'s terms: it grants authority over
     // nothing, and a process that cannot ask which tier it is running on
     // cannot make the one decision the word exists for.
     | (SystemRight.TierGet as UInt32)
     // `StatsRegionMap` is here on `SlabDonate`'s terms and not on `TierGet`'s:
     // it does leak state (every process's trap columns, in one page), so it is
     // in the root set because root is the process meant to be able to
     // supervise the machine, and it is the kind of bit a deployment strips on
     // the way down to anything that is not a supervisor.
     | (SystemRight.StatsRegionMap as UInt32)
     // `InterruptBind` is here on `SlabDonate`'s terms: a bit nothing starts
     // with is a bit nothing can hold, root is the launcher that binds every
     // line and gives each Interrupt to its driver, and a deployment strips
     // the bit on the way down to anything that is not a binder.
     | (SystemRight.InterruptBind as UInt32)
     | (SystemRight.Transfer as UInt32))
}

/// The Process default set: one set, three minters (§12).
///
/// The three places a Process handle is minted (root's at boot, the one
/// `SystemOp.ProcessSelf` hands a process for itself, and the one
/// `SystemOp.ProcessCreate` hands a launcher for its child) all mint this.
/// There is one list because two lists with nothing distinguishing them would
/// be a second place to forget a bit.
///
/// What is in it, all named for the op it gates: `ThreadCreate`, `ThreadSelf`,
/// `Exit`, `Wait`, `EventCreate`, `WaiterCreate`, `Start`,
/// `BootHandles`, `Give`, `Map`, `PipeCreate`, `Stats`, plus the two universal
/// bits `Transfer` and `Mint`.
///
/// Two bits read oddly on root's own handle and are harmless there, which is
/// the price of one list. `Start` on a process that is already `Running` is the
/// ordinary `BadState`, and nobody starts root. `Give` into a process that is
/// not `Created` is the same refusal. The state word answers both better than a
/// rights word could, because it answers them for a child too.
///
/// A process narrows the Process handle it derives through a keep mask:
/// `SystemOp.ProcessSelf` mints this set intersected with the mask the
/// deriving process passes. A launcher's mask on the System handle it gives a
/// child does not reach that handle, and a launcher has no way to narrow it:
/// only the child can drop `Map`, the bit that separates a driver from an
/// ordinary process, from the handle it derives (spec §9).
public func process_rights() -> UInt32 {
    ((ProcessRight.ThreadCreate as UInt32) | (ProcessRight.ThreadSelf as UInt32)
     | (ProcessRight.Exit as UInt32) | (ProcessRight.Wait as UInt32)
     | (ProcessRight.EventCreate as UInt32)
     | (ProcessRight.WaiterCreate as UInt32)
     | (ProcessRight.Start as UInt32)
     | (ProcessRight.BootHandles as UInt32) | (ProcessRight.Give as UInt32)
     | (ProcessRight.Map as UInt32)
     | (ProcessRight.PipeCreate as UInt32)
     | (ProcessRight.Stats as UInt32)
     | (ProcessRight.Transfer as UInt32) | (ProcessRight.Mint as UInt32))
}

/// What the kernel mints for a PipeInlet at `ProcessOp.PipeCreate` (spec §2.1).
///
/// The creator gets every end of its own connection, on `event_rights`'
/// reasoning: it made the object and there is nobody to attenuate against yet.
/// Attenuation happens when a handle is derived for somebody else (a `MINT_OP`
/// sibling through a keep mask), and §3 makes that monotonic, so a client
/// handed a post-only inlet can never widen back to this.
///
/// `Transfer` has to be here, for the reason `memory_rights` states about
/// `MapExecute`: attenuation only ever narrows, so a bit not minted at creation
/// could never appear later by any sequence of ops, and a connection whose ends
/// cannot travel has exactly one process on both sides of it, which is not IPC.
public func pipe_inlet_rights() -> UInt32 {
    ((PipeInletRight.Transfer as UInt32) | (PipeInletRight.Mint as UInt32)
     | (PipeInletRight.Post as UInt32) | (PipeInletRight.Wait as UInt32))
}

/// What the kernel mints for a PipeOutlet at `ProcessOp.PipeCreate`. The mirror
/// of `pipe_inlet_rights`, with the verb changed and nothing else.
public func pipe_outlet_rights() -> UInt32 {
    ((PipeOutletRight.Transfer as UInt32) | (PipeOutletRight.Mint as UInt32)
     | (PipeOutletRight.Take as UInt32) | (PipeOutletRight.Wait as UInt32))
}

/// What the kernel mints for a PipeReply at `PipeInletOp.Post`: the claim the
/// poster is handed (spec §2.1).
///
/// The poster gets every bit of its own claim but one, on `pipe_inlet_rights`'
/// reasoning as far as it goes. `Transfer` carries the same weight it does
/// there: §2.1 makes both one-shots transferable, so a claim that could not
/// travel would be a promise the spec makes and the kernel withholds.
///
/// The one withheld is `Mint`, because a one-shot has one name. Handing a
/// narrower claim on is `give`'s keep mask, which needs no sibling
/// (`tests/give-keep-mask`), and the sibling a mint would produce could only
/// ever be a second claim on an exchange only one act may spend. The bit is
/// declared in `PipeReplyRight` and simply not handed out here; attenuation is
/// monotonic, so no sequence of ops puts it back.
public func pipe_reply_rights() -> UInt32 {
    ((PipeReplyRight.Transfer as UInt32)
     | (PipeReplyRight.Resolve as UInt32) | (PipeReplyRight.Wait as UInt32))
}

/// What the kernel mints for a PipeRequest at `PipeOutletOp.Take`: the
/// obligation the taker is handed. The mirror of `pipe_reply_rights`, with the
/// verb changed: `Mint` is withheld here for the same one-name reason, and
/// delegation is untouched because delegation is `Transfer` moving the one name
/// rather than a second one being made (`tests/pipe-delegate-msg`).
///
/// `Transfer` here is §2.1's delegation primitive itself, not a convenience:
/// the ratified zero-copy example is a filesystem forwarding this very handle
/// to a flash driver, which replies with the hardware bytes straight to the
/// original client. Withholding the bit would delete the example.
public func pipe_request_rights() -> UInt32 {
    ((PipeRequestRight.Transfer as UInt32)
     | (PipeRequestRight.Reply as UInt32) | (PipeRequestRight.Wait as UInt32))
}

/// What the kernel mints for a Memory region: a boot RAM-pool root, and every
/// region a `Split` derives from one (spec §2.5).
///
/// One set, two minters, on `process_rights()`'s precedent. A boot root and a
/// split child carry the same bits, because a split is a derivation and §3
/// makes derivation monotonic: a caller that wants a narrower child mints an
/// attenuated sibling of it (`MINT_OP`) and hands that on. Two default sets
/// here would be a second place to forget a bit, and would silently make "the
/// second cut of a pool" less capable than the first.
///
/// The kind-specific bits are named for the ops they gate (`Split`, `Map`,
/// `MapExecute`) rather than a generic authority.
///
/// `MapExecute` is in the set, and it has to be. §3's attenuation is monotonic:
/// a bit not minted at boot could never appear later by any sequence of ops, so
/// a default set that withheld it would make an executable mapping unreachable
/// in the whole system rather than merely restricted. The narrowing is the
/// holder's, done with `MINT_OP` and a keep mask that omits the bit.
///
/// So the default is permissive and the policy is written where the capability
/// is handed over, not here. Root holds everything, and what a child holds is
/// what root chose to mint.
public func memory_rights() -> UInt32 {
    ((MemoryRight.Transfer as UInt32) | (MemoryRight.Mint as UInt32)
     | (MemoryRight.Split as UInt32) | (MemoryRight.Map as UInt32)
     | (MemoryRight.MapExecute as UInt32))
}

/// What the kernel mints for an IoMemory window: a boot device-pool root, and
/// every window a `Carve` derives from one (spec §2.5).
///
/// The same one-set-two-minters shape `memory_rights` has, and for the same
/// reason. `Transfer` is what lets root hand a driver child its window, and it
/// is minted at boot because attenuation only ever narrows.
///
/// It gains no `MapExecute`: a device window has no access argument to gate, so
/// the authority would name an ask nobody can make.
public func iomemory_rights() -> UInt32 {
    ((IoMemoryRight.Transfer as UInt32) | (IoMemoryRight.Mint as UInt32)
     | (IoMemoryRight.Carve as UInt32) | (IoMemoryRight.Map as UInt32))
}

/// What the kernel mints for a Mapping a process has just installed (spec
/// §2.5).
///
/// `Transfer` is absent by silence, the one thing this set says that the others
/// do not: a Mapping names a row in one particular process's domain, so it is
/// not a capability that travels. `MappingRight` records the argument. `Mint`
/// is granted on the uniform lean the section above states: a second name for a
/// mapping costs nothing and can itself be attenuated, which is how a holder
/// hands out an un-unmappable reference to one.
public func mapping_rights() -> UInt32 {
    ((MappingRight.Unmap as UInt32) | (MappingRight.Mint as UInt32)
     | (MappingRight.Base as UInt32))
}

/// What the kernel mints for a thread's own handle: root's initial thread at
/// boot, and every thread `Process.ThreadCreate` makes.
///
/// `Mint` is granted on the uniform lean the default-sets section records. The
/// kind-specific bits are the whole of the object's surface. A thread's creator
/// holds a handle and the thread can mint its own through `ThreadSelf`, and
/// both carry this same set, so there is no attenuated split between the two to
/// express. `ThreadSelf` mints a fresh handle on every call, so "both hold a
/// handle" means two independently owned instances rather than one word read
/// twice.
///
/// `Transfer` is in the set because a creation op places the new handle in the
/// caller's table and the caller gives it on (§2.7), at boot or in a message,
/// to whoever joins the thread. A Thread is bound to the process it runs in:
/// a handle another process holds outlives that process's death, and reads the
/// exit code if the thread exited first or `Revoked` if it did not (§2.10).
public func thread_rights() -> UInt32 {
    ((ThreadRight.Transfer as UInt32) | (ThreadRight.Start as UInt32)
     | (ThreadRight.Join as UInt32) | (ThreadRight.Control as UInt32)
     | (ThreadRight.Mint as UInt32))
}

/// What the kernel mints for an Event a process has just created (§2.4).
///
/// The creator gets every end of its own notification: it made the object, so
/// there is nobody to attenuate against yet. Attenuation happens when a handle
/// is derived for somebody else, and §3 makes that monotonic, so a producer
/// handed `Signal` alone can never widen back to this.
///
/// `Transfer` is in the set because a creation op places the new handle in the
/// caller's table and the caller gives it on (§2.7): a producer is handed
/// `Signal` by a give with a keep mask, and a message may carry an Event. An
/// Event is a shared kind, so it outlives the process that made it for as long
/// as anything names it (§2.10).
public func event_rights() -> UInt32 {
    ((EventRight.Transfer as UInt32) | (EventRight.Signal as UInt32)
     | (EventRight.Receive as UInt32) | (EventRight.Wait as UInt32)
     | (EventRight.Mint as UInt32))
}

/// What the kernel mints for a Waiter a process has just created (§2.2).
///
/// `Transfer` is in the set because a creation op places the new handle in the
/// caller's table and the caller gives it on (§2.7), at boot or in a message.
/// A Waiter is bound to the process that created it: a handle another process
/// holds outlives that process's death, every thread parked on the Waiter then
/// wakes with `Revoked`, and every later op through the handle answers it
/// (§2.10).
public func waiter_rights() -> UInt32 {
    ((WaiterRight.Transfer as UInt32) | (WaiterRight.Attach as UInt32)
     | (WaiterRight.Wait as UInt32) | (WaiterRight.Mint as UInt32))
}

/// What the kernel mints for an Interrupt a process has just bound (§9).
///
/// Both ends of the cycle, for the reason `event_rights` gives: the binder
/// made the object and there is nobody to attenuate against yet. `Transfer` is
/// in the set so that a launcher can hand a driver its line.
///
/// `Mint` is not, for the reason `pipe_reply_rights` withholds it: an Interrupt
/// has one name, so a sibling could only be a second name for a line that one
/// handle destroys when it is released. Attenuation is monotonic, so no
/// sequence of ops puts the bit back. A masked copy for a monitor is a give's
/// keep mask.
public func interrupt_rights() -> UInt32 {
    ((InterruptRight.Transfer as UInt32) | (InterruptRight.Wait as UInt32)
     | (InterruptRight.Ack as UInt32))
}

/// What the kernel mints for a Clock a process has just been granted (§2
/// table).
///
/// Both ends, for the reason `event_rights` gives: the grant is the whole
/// capability and there is nobody to attenuate against yet. A child handed a
/// read-only clock is a derivation, and §3 makes derivation monotonic, so it
/// can never widen back to this.
public func clock_rights() -> UInt32 {
    ((ClockRight.Read as UInt32) | (ClockRight.TimerCreate as UInt32)
     | (ClockRight.Mint as UInt32))
}

/// What the kernel mints for a Timer a process has just created (§2 table).
///
/// `Transfer` is in the set for `waiter_rights`' reason. A Timer is bound to
/// the process that created it: its owner's death disarms it for good, an
/// attachment on it fires with its ordinary record, and every later `Arm` or
/// `Disarm` through a surviving handle answers `Revoked` (§2.10).
public func timer_rights() -> UInt32 {
    ((TimerRight.Transfer as UInt32) | (TimerRight.Arm as UInt32)
     | (TimerRight.Wait as UInt32) | (TimerRight.Mint as UInt32))
}
